#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068532 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee633017-5c17-575f-bdab-5f12b7319355" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Area Between Two Curves as an Integral Given the Limits of Integration"}] :question/id #uuid "481f1075-efc3-546a-a1f3-9415f7fc642d" :question/math-academy-id "e-552" :question/problem "Write down an integral that gives the area of the finite region bounded by the curves $y=x^2$ and $y=\\sqrt x$, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/395/Source/Images/0af8c0419c649a2986c7a4112399a532.png)" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the intersection points between the two graphs occur when $x=0$ and $x=1$. So these will be our limits of integration.\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/395/Source/Images/e2dad27370be8b6a9b82b1adc02d06c5.png)\n\nThe vertical line\n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=x^2$, and\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=\\sqrt x$.\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= ∫_{0}^{1}[(upper function) - (lower function)]dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}[(\\sqrt{x}) - (x^{2})]dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}(\\sqrt{x} - x^{2})dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068533 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8fa1bebf-4af0-5a5c-962c-8a88f8392776" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Area Between Two Curves Expressed as Functions of X"}] :question/id #uuid "ff023940-30f8-5b30-ab1b-9ca1f335a6a8" :question/math-academy-id "e-553" :question/problem "Find the area of the finite region enclosed between $f(x) =x^2-2x+2$ and $g(x) = 2$, shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/395/Source/Images/5c6f110a048606921590ea4e6403ede0.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 2x + 2 &= 2 \\\\\nx^{2} - 2x &= 0 \\\\\nx(x - 2) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=0$ to $x=2$.\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, we draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/395/Source/Images/88219aab4954daef055e1fa24e9e55b6.png)\n\nThe vertical line\n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=x^2-2x+2$, and\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=2$.\n\nTherefore, we can find the area as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= ∫_{0}^{2}[(upper function) - (lower function)]dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{2}[(2) - (x^{2} - 2x + 2)]dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{2}(-x^{2} + 2x)dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{2}(2x - x^{2})dx \\\\\n&= (x^{2} - \\frac{1}{3}x^{3}) \\mid _{0}^{2} \\\\\n&= (2^{2} - \\frac{1}{3} \\cdot 2^{3}) - (0 - 0) \\\\\n&= (4 - \\frac{8}{3}) - 0 \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068534 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e016cedd-0206-555c-8934-c6d19566d547" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Area Between Two Curves as an Integral by First Solving For the Limits of Integration"}] :question/id #uuid "d1c021cd-b537-5504-8836-eb7a59ba86b6" :question/math-academy-id "e-554" :question/problem "What is the integral that gives the area of the finite region bounded by $y=x^2+x+5$ and $y=x+9$?" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw a sketch of the two functions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/395/Source/Images/36b16b895b0234f1f9e10a91977d6c66.png)\n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*\n\nNow, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + x + 5 &= x + 9 \\\\\nx^{2} - 4 &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 2)(x + 2) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=-2$ to $x=2$.\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/395/Source/Images/c27aaed348fa7bebc01f25f85b3a720e.png)\n\nThe vertical line\n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=x^2+x+5$, and\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=x+9$.\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= ∫_{-2}^{2}[(upper function) - (lower function)]dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{-2}^{2}[(x + 9) - (x^{2} + x + 5)]dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{-2}^{2}(4 - x^{2})dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069313 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e016cedd-0206-555c-8934-c6d19566d547" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Area Between Two Curves as an Integral by First Solving For the Limits of Integration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "968c81d4-3952-5546-bf6e-2fa86c99edd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{-1}^{2/5}(-5x^{2} - 3x + 2)dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067410} {:answer/id #uuid "ffcf879e-89d4-552d-841b-265a1abe4bdc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{-5}^{2}(2 - x^{2} - 4x)dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067411} {:answer/id #uuid "1d25566a-112d-528b-8bb3-9ee467995089" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{-5}^{2}(4x^{2} - x)dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067412} {:answer/id #uuid "55169664-5c10-50fa-bb89-ca3fd3db0275" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{-1}^{2/5}(2 - x^{2} - 4x)dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067413} {:answer/id #uuid "77e1737d-81b3-5bac-be4d-95f8051dd21e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{-1}^{2/5}(4x^{2} - x)dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067414}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "968c81d4-3952-5546-bf6e-2fa86c99edd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{-1}^{2/5}(-5x^{2} - 3x + 2)dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067410} :answer-field/id #uuid "1961923d-7ea2-5d20-b11d-3cfa8db69ef6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067409}] :question/id #uuid "d663ded3-4e3e-5b80-80de-bcbc2d02a27f" :question/math-academy-id "q-49885" :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nWhich integral gives the area of the finite region bounded by $y = 4x^{2} - x$ and $y = 2 - x^{2} - 4x$?" :question/requires-calculator true}]}
